1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,317
The brush uncovers a coin with writing on both sides, and the writing is in two different scripts.

2
00:00:06,317 --> 00:00:09,954
Somebody wanted to be understood by two populations at once.

3
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:14,330
For the next three centuries,

4
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:20,398
the north-west is where that keeps happening — and coins are often the only evidence we have that a king existed at all.

5
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:26,496
Coins carry this episode.

6
00:00:26,496 --> 00:00:32,264
Some of these rulers appear in no text at all and are known only because their coins survive.

7
00:00:32,264 --> 00:00:37,842
Inscriptions add a little, and Indian and classical texts add names and a few events.

8
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:47,454
Four groups come in, roughly in order, and the order is the thing to learn.

9
00:00:47,454 --> 00:00:51,646
The Indo-Greeks, descended from Alexander's successors in Bactria.

10
00:00:51,646 --> 00:00:54,510
Then the Shakas, often called Scythians.

11
00:00:57,167 --> 00:01:00,108
Then the Parthians, sometimes called Pahlavas.

12
00:01:00,108 --> 00:01:02,988
Then the Kushanas, who get their own episode.

13
00:01:02,988 --> 00:01:06,605
Each pushes the one before it further east or south.

14
00:01:13,833 --> 00:01:15,979
The Indo-Greeks matter for two reasons.

15
00:01:15,979 --> 00:01:22,394
Their coins are the first in India to carry a ruler's portrait and name, which is why we can date things by them.

16
00:01:26,267 --> 00:01:31,726
And the best known of them, Menander — Milinda in Indian sources — appears in a Buddhist text,

17
00:01:31,726 --> 00:01:36,611
the Milindapanha, as a king who questions the monk Nagasena about Buddhist doctrine.

18
00:01:36,611 --> 00:01:42,773
A Greek king arguing philosophy in an Indian Buddhist text tells you how mixed this world had become.

19
00:01:47,467 --> 00:01:55,275
The Shakas ruled in several branches; the most important for the exam is the western one, the Western Kshatrapas, based in Ujjain.

20
00:01:58,733 --> 00:02:06,792
Their best-known ruler is Rudradaman the First, and his Junagadh rock inscription is a landmark — it is in polished Sanskrit rather than Prakrit,

21
00:02:06,792 --> 00:02:13,746
which is early for a royal inscription, and it records his repair of the Sudarshana lake, the Mauryan dam from the last unit.

22
00:02:13,746 --> 00:02:17,854
Two dynasties, four centuries apart, maintaining the same reservoir.

23
00:02:25,567 --> 00:02:28,536
Two more things this period leaves behind.

24
00:02:28,536 --> 00:02:35,235
An era: the Shaka era, beginning in 78 CE, which India adopted as its national calendar in 1957.

25
00:02:35,235 --> 00:02:43,454
And a style: in the north-west, Gandhara, where sculptors trained in Hellenistic conventions carved Buddhist subjects,

26
00:02:43,454 --> 00:02:47,483
producing figures with flowing, naturalistic drapery.

27
00:02:52,067 --> 00:02:56,623
Around the same time Mathura develops its own idiom from Indian traditions.

28
00:02:56,623 --> 00:03:02,319
We are showing drapery and ornament here, not figures, in keeping with this series' rule.

29
00:03:06,333 --> 00:03:07,305
A naming note.

30
00:03:07,305 --> 00:03:13,432
Shakari, a title claimed by more than one Indian ruler, simply means enemy of the Shakas.

31
00:03:19,567 --> 00:03:22,599
Milinda in the Buddhist text is the Greek Menander.

32
00:03:22,599 --> 00:03:23,998
Pahlava means Parthian.

33
00:03:23,998 --> 00:03:28,905
Questions often test whether you can match the Indian name to the foreign one.

34
00:03:31,567 --> 00:03:34,718
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:34,718 --> 00:03:39,092
Greeks, Shakas, Parthians, Kushanas — in that order.

36
00:03:43,933 --> 00:03:44,618
Dig Quiz.

37
00:03:44,618 --> 00:03:45,920
Pause and name it.

38
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:51,400
First: the Buddhist text in which a Greek king questions a monk about doctrine.

39
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:52,933
The Milindapanha.

40
00:03:58,033 --> 00:04:03,085
Second: the rock inscription in Sanskrit recording the repair of the Sudarshana lake.

41
00:04:03,085 --> 00:04:06,322
The Junagadh inscription of Rudradaman the First.

42
00:04:08,967 --> 00:04:15,037
Third: the era beginning in 78 CE that India later adopted as its national calendar.

43
00:04:15,037 --> 00:04:16,109
The Shaka era.

44
00:04:16,109 --> 00:04:20,694
In Mains, a good question asks what these contacts changed.

45
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:30,951
Point to coinage, to Sanskrit entering royal inscriptions, to Gandhara, and to the trade routes that came with the rulers.

46
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:42,103
A coin in two scripts takes its place in the gallery: the first money in India with a face and a name on it.

47
00:04:46,067 --> 00:04:51,659
Next gallery: the dynasty that turned the north-west into a crossroads of the whole known world.

48
00:04:51,659 --> 00:04:55,245
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

